many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism  why does Stoics focus on negative  visualization this is an excellent  question particularly given that one of  the promises of stoic philosophy for its  practitioners is that ultimately they're  going to be living a life that is as  free and untroubled as possible free of  negative emotions so it's a little  paradoxical isn't it to think that by  focusing on the negative on what could  go wrong on what might be heading our  way that we would get rid of these  negative emotions there are very clear  benefits to engaging in negative  visualization regularly and we're going  to talk about those in a moment but  before that let's get clear on what  we're actually talking about when we use  these terms negative visualization  especially since if you read through  classic stomach texts you're not going  to find that term anywhere instead what  you're going to see is the Stoics  telling you to for example keep death  before your eyes and you know things are  going to go well with you or before you  go into a situation think about this  sort of thing or consider all these  different possibilities or when you go  to kiss your wife or child remind  yourself that they are a mortal being  and will die and then you won't be quite  so upset when it happens which may  strike us as very morbid or even cruel  so what's going on here are the Stoics  indulging in a sort of thinking negative  thoughts for the sake of thinking  negative thoughts are they all Downers  are they all people who are pessimistic  no that's not the case and I want to say  something about the opposite extreme -  this is not some sort  homeopathy of the mind where you're  taking just a tiny tiny little bit of  negativity almost a trace amount and  that will clear out all the other things  that you're going to feel sort of the  analogy of homeopathy no you're going to  actually think about you're going to  consider you're going to visualize  things that could go wrong or will  eventually happen that we typically view  as negative from the perspective of  stoic philosophy these are not always  negative but from the perspective of the  larger culture both in ancient times and  in our present these are viewed as bad  if you're at all unclear about what  we're talking about maybe a few concrete  examples could be helpful so imagine  that you're heading into some kind of  contest like an athletic contest you're  playing a game against somebody and  there's some stakes maybe you've put  some money on the game maybe people that  you care about are watching you and want  you to win  maybe you just are naturally competitive  person and you think about what could go  wrong within the game and all the  different ins and outs you know you you  think about your opponents and how  you're going to face that more if it's  and you know something like doing a long  jump you think about the conditions of  the track and you run this through your  mind not necessarily for a long time but  before you actually engage in it that  would be negative visualization thinking  about the possible loss of loved ones as  I just brought up with epictetus is  another one we you're in a relationship  with somebody and you think I'm so happy  that I have this this new relationship  my life feels complete and you remind  yourself that it could end at any moment  that they could be taken away from you  for all sorts of different reasons  that's negative visualization as well  imagine that you've got a very important  meeting an interview let's say a job  interview you're going in  and you're going to try to present  yourself in the best way and you think  about the sorts of questions that might  get asked that could stump you or you  consider the matters that you're a bit  embarrassed about in your CV or your  resume and whether they're gonna probe  about that and we could go on and on all  the different things you know what  happens if you spill coffee on your  shirt what happens if you walk in and  it's very clear that you're overdressed  or underdressed how are you going to  handle it these are all negative  visualization I'd mentioned that there  are some benefits to engaging a negative  visualization and this is exactly why  the Stoics advocated as a practical  exercise to engage in on a broad scale  daily but also to do when we're heading  into difficult situations so what are  those benefits if you're engaging in  negative visualization ahead of time  when you actually get to the potentially  disturbing event you will find more  times than not that it may still feel  bad but it doesn't feel as bad as if you  hadn't done the negative visualization  so that's a very important benefit  obviously the further along you get on  the stomach path the less that that you  would be disturbed by things but for the  rest of us this is very important  another thing that it helps you to do is  to think about the possible outcomes and  to have a certain sense of perspective  on the probability of those quite often  the really catastrophic things that we  fear or are averse to they're not that  likely to happen and by reflecting on  that you may feel a bit better about it  it's usually the smaller scale  annoyances that are more likely to  happen and then you can focus your  energy on that and that brings us to the  third benefit which is when you think  about what could happen  and how things might go from your  perspective wrong you can also think  about what resources you would have to  deal with the situation what would you  need to do in order to be a little bit  more prepared and even thinking about  that preparation can sometimes give you  the edge so that when you're in the  actual situation time bound you can  respond in real time in a way that's  effective for you in that situation  finally the last part is you can also  think about let's say this bad thing  does happen what's still the case it  allows you to appreciate what you still  do have you know when somebody has a  consoling thing well at least you have  your health now health of course is a  preferred indifferent for Stoics but  that's still something you know quite  good something useful that that's an  observation that can be very helpful  within the situation so negative  visualization can help us in each of  these four ways and it does some other  things as well where would you go if you  want to learn more about this as I  remarked if you go to the classic stoic  text which I highly recommend you're  gonna find them referring to this many  times but not using the word negative  visualization you're also not going to  see them using another term which comes  about later on pramatta taught Co  malorum the again the thinking ahead of  time about the bad things that that  could happen but if you do read you know  Marcus Aurelius  Epictetus Seneca you'll see them talking  a great deal about concrete examples  general principles and how to do this if  you want to read the more modern stoic  literature there's plenty of books out  there all you have to do is type in  negative visualization here you see a  number of blog entries and videos come  up as well so there's a there's a vast  amount of literature and other material  out there that can help you with this  but it is something that you have to  practice you get better at it the more  you do it if you found this answer  helpful for you then please share it  with others who might benefit from it  this work is entirely supported by  crowdfunding on my patreon site so if  you find it valuable consider becoming a  supporter at patreon.com slash Sadler
 

